---
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
import RelatedTools from '../../../components/RelatedTools.astro';

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', href: '/' },
  { name: 'Tools', href: '/tools/' },
  { name: 'Nutrition & Body Composition', href: '/tools/nutrition/' },
  { name: 'Body Fat Calculator' },
];

/*
 * NOTE: FAQ answers may include HTML links for the visible page (rendered via
 * set:html). Base.astro strips HTML before injecting into FAQPage JSON-LD.
 * Keep links to internal tools/authoritative sources only — no inline styles.
 */
const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'How accurate is the Navy body fat calculator compared with DEXA?',
    answer:
      'The Navy circumference method is a field estimate, not a laboratory measurement. A 2022 study of physically fit U.S. Marines found average underestimation of about 2.5–2.6 percentage points in men and overestimation of about 1.3–2.3 percentage points in women, with individual variability of roughly 3.7–4.8 percentage points. DEXA is often used as a reference standard in research, but its own accuracy also depends on the device, software version, and population studied.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What is a healthy body fat percentage?',
    answer:
      'Body fat ranges vary by sex, age, population, and reference source. Common fitness references often describe approximately 18–24% for men and 25–31% for women as typical non-athlete ranges. These are not universal medical thresholds, and healthy ranges tend to shift upward with age as muscle mass naturally declines.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Why is body fat percentage different from BMI?',
    answer:
      'BMI uses only height and body weight and cannot distinguish fat mass from muscle mass. Body fat percentage attempts to estimate the proportion of body weight that is fat, although the Navy method is itself an estimate. A person with high muscle mass can have a high BMI but low body fat, while someone with low muscle mass can have a normal BMI but high body fat ("skinny fat").',
  },
  {
    question: 'Where should I measure my waist, neck, and hips?',
    answer:
      'For the Navy-style circumference method, men measure the abdomen at the navel and the neck below the larynx. Women measure the natural waist at its smallest point, the hips at the greatest protrusion, and the neck below the larynx. Keep the tape level and snug without compressing the skin. Use a flexible tape measure rather than a rigid metal one.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can I use this body fat calculator for men and women?',
    answer:
      'Yes. The calculator uses separate Navy circumference equations for males and females. Women also enter a hip measurement because the female equation uses waist, hip, and neck circumferences. The BMI-based estimate uses sex as one of its variables.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Is the Navy body fat method better than a smart scale?',
    answer:
      'Neither method should automatically be treated as a precise measurement for an individual. Bioelectrical impedance (BIA) smart scales can be affected significantly by hydration, recent meals, and sweat. The Navy tape method depends on tape placement and technique. For tracking progress, using the same method and conditions over time tends to be more useful than comparing absolute numbers across different methods.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Why does my smart scale show a different body fat % than this calculator?',
    answer:
      'Smart scales use Bioelectrical Impedance Analysis (BIA), which sends a small current through your body. BIA is highly sensitive to hydration, recent meals, and sweat, and consumer devices can vary meaningfully from one measurement to the next. The Navy method relies on physical circumferences, so the two methods can naturally produce different estimates on the same person.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can I have a normal BMI but still have too much body fat?',
    answer:
      'Yes. This is known as "normal-weight obesity" or the "skinny fat" phenotype. If you have low muscle mass but carry excess visceral fat, your BMI might be normal while your body fat percentage remains in a higher range. Research suggests this pattern is associated with increased risk of insulin resistance, dyslipidemia, and cardiovascular disease.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What is the difference between subcutaneous and visceral fat?',
    answer:
      'Subcutaneous fat sits just under your skin and is the fat you can pinch. Visceral fat sits deeper in the abdomen surrounding your internal organs and has been linked to higher metabolic health risks, including high blood pressure, high cholesterol, and high blood sugar. A waist over 40 inches (101.6 cm) for men or 35 inches (88.9 cm) for non-pregnant women is often used as a signal of elevated risk.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Is body fat percentage better than BMI?',
    answer:
      'For assessing body composition, body fat percentage is generally more informative than BMI alone. BMI only looks at height and weight and cannot distinguish between muscle and fat. Body fat percentage estimates your fat-to-lean mass ratio, which is more useful for fitness and metabolic health tracking. However, both are estimates when measured outside a clinical setting.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How can I lower my body fat percentage?',
    answer:
      'Reducing body fat generally involves sustained energy balance, appropriate nutrition, resistance training, physical activity, and adequate recovery. A calorie target can be estimated with a TDEE calculator, while protein and macro targets can be set with dedicated calculators. Use our <a href="/tools/nutrition/tdee-calculator/">TDEE Calculator</a> and <a href="/tools/nutrition/protein-calculator/">Protein Calculator</a> to build a practical plan.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What should I calculate after body fat percentage?',
    answer:
      'If you are using body composition to plan nutrition, you can estimate daily calorie needs with a <a href="/tools/nutrition/tdee-calculator/">TDEE Calculator</a> and then use a <a href="/tools/nutrition/macro-calculator/">Macro Calculator</a> to create a calorie and macronutrient target.',
  },
];

const appSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'WebApplication',
  '@id': 'https://repmaths.com/tools/nutrition/body-fat-calculator/#calculator',
  name: 'Body Fat Calculator (US Navy Method)',
  alternateName: 'Body Fat Percentage Calculator',
  url: 'https://repmaths.com/tools/nutrition/body-fat-calculator/',
  description:
    'Free body fat percentage calculator using the US Navy tape-measure method. Enter neck, waist, and height (plus hip for women) in inches or cm for an instant estimate.',
  applicationCategory: 'HealthApplication',
  operatingSystem: 'Any',
  browserRequirements: 'Requires JavaScript',
  inLanguage: 'en',
  isAccessibleForFree: true,
  offers: {
    '@type': 'Offer',
    price: '0',
    priceCurrency: 'USD',
  },
  publisher: {
    '@id': 'https://repmaths.com/#organization',
  },
  isPartOf: {
    '@id': 'https://repmaths.com/#website',
  },
};

const jsonLdSafe = (value: unknown) =>
  JSON.stringify(value).replace(/</g, '\\u003c');
---

<Base
  title="Body Fat Calculator (US Navy Method) — Men & Women"
  description="Free body fat percentage calculator using the US Navy tape-measure method. Enter neck, waist, and height (plus hip for women) in inches or cm for an instant estimate."
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <script
    type="application/ld+json"
    set:html={jsonLdSafe(appSchema)}
  />

  <div class="tool-page">
    <header class="tool-header">
      <p class="eyebrow">Nutrition &amp; Body Composition</p>
      <h1>Body Fat Calculator (US Navy Method)</h1>

      <p class="intro">
        Estimate your body fat percentage from height and tape-measure readings
        using the U.S. Navy circumference method. You can also use the BMI-based
        estimate if you don't have a tape measure.
      </p>
    </header>

    <aside class="quick-answer" aria-label="Body fat quick answer">
      <p>
        <strong>What is a healthy body fat percentage?</strong> Common fitness
        references describe roughly <strong>18–24% for men</strong> and
        <strong>25–31% for women</strong> as typical non-athlete ranges. The
        U.S. Navy tape method estimates body fat from neck, waist, and height
        (plus hip for women) and is a field estimate — individual accuracy
        varies by technique and population.
      </p>
    </aside>

    <section class="calculator-card" aria-labelledby="calculator-heading">
      <h2 id="calculator-heading">Calculate Your Body Fat Percentage</h2>

      <div class="field full">
        <label>Calculation method</label>
        <div class="unit-toggle" role="group" aria-label="Calculation method">
          <button type="button" class="unit-btn active" data-method="navy">Navy Tape Method</button>
          <button type="button" class="unit-btn" data-method="bmi">BMI Estimate</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="form-grid">
        <div class="field">
          <label>Unit system</label>
          <div class="unit-toggle" role="group" aria-label="Unit system">
            <button type="button" class="unit-btn active" data-unit="imperial">Imperial (in, lb)</button>
            <button type="button" class="unit-btn" data-unit="metric">Metric (cm, kg)</button>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="field">
          <label>Sex</label>
          <div class="unit-toggle" role="group" aria-label="Sex">
            <button type="button" class="unit-btn active" data-sex="male">Male</button>
            <button type="button" class="unit-btn" data-sex="female">Female</button>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="field">
          <label for="height" id="height-label">Height (in)</label>
          <div class="input-with-unit">
            <input id="height" type="number" placeholder="e.g. 70" inputmode="decimal" step="0.1" autocomplete="off" aria-describedby="error" />
            <span id="height-unit-span">in</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Navy method inputs -->
        <div class="field" id="neck-field">
          <label for="neck" id="neck-label">Neck (in, below larynx)</label>
          <div class="input-with-unit">
            <input id="neck" type="number" placeholder="e.g. 15" inputmode="decimal" step="0.1" autocomplete="off" aria-describedby="error" />
            <span id="neck-unit-span">in</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="field" id="waist-field">
          <label for="waist" id="waist-label">Waist (in, at navel)</label>
          <div class="input-with-unit">
            <input id="waist" type="number" placeholder="e.g. 34" inputmode="decimal" step="0.1" autocomplete="off" aria-describedby="error" />
            <span id="waist-unit-span">in</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="field" id="hip-field" hidden>
          <label for="hip" id="hip-label">Hip (in, widest point)</label>
          <div class="input-with-unit">
            <input id="hip" type="number" placeholder="e.g. 38" inputmode="decimal" step="0.1" autocomplete="off" aria-describedby="error" />
            <span id="hip-unit-span">in</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Weight — always visible. Navy = optional; BMI = required -->
        <div class="field" id="weight-field">
          <label for="weight" id="weight-label">Weight (lb, optional)</label>
          <div class="input-with-unit">
            <input id="weight" type="number" placeholder="e.g. 160" inputmode="decimal" step="0.1" autocomplete="off" aria-describedby="error" />
            <span id="weight-unit-span">lb</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="field" id="age-field" hidden>
          <label for="age">Age</label>
          <div class="input-with-unit">
            <input id="age" type="number" placeholder="e.g. 30" inputmode="numeric" min="18" max="90" step="1" autocomplete="off" aria-describedby="error" />
            <span>years</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p id="error" class="message error" role="alert" hidden></p>

      <div id="results" class="results">
        <div
          id="result-announcement"
          class="sr-only"
          role="status"
          aria-live="polite"
          aria-atomic="true"
        ></div>

        <div class="result-primary">
          <span class="result-label">Estimated body fat percentage</span>
          <strong id="result-bf">—</strong>
          <span class="result-unit" id="result-category"></span>
        </div>

        <div class="result-grid" id="mass-grid" hidden>
          <div class="result-box">
            <span>Fat mass</span>
            <strong id="fat-mass">—</strong>
            <small id="fat-mass-unit">lb</small>
          </div>
          <div class="result-box">
            <span>Lean mass</span>
            <strong id="lean-mass">—</strong>
            <small id="lean-mass-unit">lb</small>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p id="result-note" class="calculator-note">
          This is an estimate, not a direct measurement of body fat. Individual
          accuracy varies with measurement technique, population, and the
          reference method used.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p id="warning" class="message warning" hidden></p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>How to Measure for the Navy Body Fat Calculator</h2>

      <p>
        Measurement technique matters because small differences in tape
        placement or tension can change the estimate. Use a flexible tape
        measure and take each measurement consistently.
      </p>

      <ol>
        <li>
          Use a soft measuring tape rather than a rigid metal tape.
        </li>
        <li>
          Measure the neck just below the larynx. Keep your head facing
          straight ahead and avoid pressing the tape into the skin.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Men:</strong> measure the abdomen at the level of the navel.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Women:</strong> measure the natural waist at the smallest
          portion of the abdomen.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Women only:</strong> measure the hips around the greatest
          protrusion of the gluteal area.
        </li>
        <li>
          Keep the tape approximately level and snug without compressing
          the soft tissue.
        </li>
        <li>
          Take measurements in a relaxed state rather than flexing or
          deliberately pulling your stomach in.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <p>
        For progress tracking, try to measure under similar conditions each
        time — ideally in the morning, before eating, and after using the
        restroom. Consistency is often more useful than treating a single
        circumference result as an exact body-fat measurement.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>The U.S. Navy Body Fat Formula</h2>

      <p>
        This calculator uses the circumference-based equations associated with
        the U.S. Navy method, originally developed by Hodgdon and Beckett
        (1984). The underlying equations use measurements in inches and
        logarithms.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Men:</strong><br />
        BF% = 86.010 × log₁₀(waist − neck)
        − 70.041 × log₁₀(height) + 36.76
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Women:</strong><br />
        BF% = 163.205 × log₁₀(waist + hip − neck)
        − 97.684 × log₁₀(height) − 78.387
      </p>

      <p>
        The calculator converts centimetre inputs to inches before applying
        the equations, so the same underlying formula is used regardless of
        the selected display unit.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>The BMI-Based Body Fat Formula (Deurenberg Equation)</h2>

      <p>
        The BMI Estimate method uses the Deurenberg equation, a widely cited
        adult-population formula that estimates body fat percentage from body
        mass index, age, and sex.
      </p>

      <div class="formula-box">
        <strong>BF%</strong> = 1.2 × BMI + 0.23 × Age − 10.8 × Sex − 5.4
        <br />
        <small>Sex: male = 1, female = 0</small>
      </div>

      <p>
        The formula was derived from adult population data, so it may be less
        accurate for athletes, very lean individuals, people with high muscle
        mass, and some ethnic populations. It is best treated as a quick
        fallback estimate when circumference measurements are not available.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Navy Tape Method vs. BMI Estimate</h2>

      <p>
        Both methods on this page provide estimates, but they are suited to
        different situations.
      </p>

      <div class="comparison-table-wrap">
        <table>
          <caption>Choosing between the Navy tape method and the BMI estimate</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Factor</th>
              <th scope="col">Navy Tape Method</th>
              <th scope="col">BMI Estimate</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>What you need</td>
              <td>Tape measure, height</td>
              <td>Scale, height, age</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Where it works best</td>
              <td>Most non-athlete adults</td>
              <td>Quick estimate without a tape</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Main limitation</td>
              <td>Technique-sensitive; can misclassify very muscular or very lean individuals</td>
              <td>Does not distinguish muscle from fat; less accurate for athletes and very muscular people</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Typical use case</td>
              <td>Progress tracking when a tape is available</td>
              <td>Fallback when only a scale is available</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        If you have a tape measure, the Navy method is generally the better
        choice for tracking body composition over time. If you don't, the BMI
        estimate can provide a rough starting point but should not be treated
        as a precise body-fat measurement.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Body Fat Percentage Ranges by Age and Sex</h2>

      <p>
        Body-fat percentage categories vary by organization, age, sex, and
        population. The following age-adjusted ranges are based on
        <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10966886/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Gallagher et al. (2000)</a>,
        a peer-reviewed analysis published in the <em>American Journal of
        Clinical Nutrition</em> that proposed healthy body fat ranges based on
        BMI cutoffs using the four-compartment model.
      </p>

      <div class="comparison-table-wrap">
        <table>
          <caption>Category reference ranges for non-athlete adults</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Category</th>
              <th scope="col">Men</th>
              <th scope="col">Women</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Essential Fat</td>
              <td>2–5%</td>
              <td>10–13%</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Athletes</td>
              <td>6–13%</td>
              <td>14–20%</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Fitness</td>
              <td>14–17%</td>
              <td>21–24%</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Typical Non-Athlete</td>
              <td>18–24%</td>
              <td>25–31%</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Obese</td>
              <td>25%+</td>
              <td>32%+</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h3>Age-Adjusted Healthy Ranges</h3>

      <p>
        Gallagher et al. (2000) proposed the following provisional healthy
        body fat ranges for white adults, derived from BMI-based predictions
        using a four-compartment model.
      </p>

      <div class="comparison-table-wrap">
        <table>
          <caption>Healthy body fat ranges by age (Gallagher et al., 2000)</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Age</th>
              <th scope="col">Men</th>
              <th scope="col">Women</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>20–39</td>
              <td>8–19%</td>
              <td>21–32%</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>40–59</td>
              <td>11–21%</td>
              <td>23–33%</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>60–79</td>
              <td>13–24%</td>
              <td>24–35%</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="calculator-note">
        These ranges are reference categories, not individual medical
        diagnoses. Very low body fat can also carry health risks, and a
        single percentage should not be used to judge overall health.
        Gallagher et al. note that their ranges are provisional and may
        need adjustment for different ethnic populations.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Body Fat Percentage vs. BMI</h2>

      <p>
        BMI uses height and total body weight. It does not directly distinguish
        muscle mass from fat mass, which can make it less informative for
        people with substantial muscular development. Body-fat percentage
        attempts to describe body composition more directly, although methods
        such as the Navy calculator are still estimates.
      </p>

      <p>
        For that reason, body fat percentage and BMI answer different
        questions. BMI is useful as a population-level screening measure,
        while body-composition estimates can provide additional context for
        individual fitness tracking. In one widely cited study from the
        University of Florida, a notable share of adults with a normal BMI
        but high body fat met criteria for prediabetes or diabetes — a
        reminder that BMI alone does not capture metabolic risk.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Smart Scale vs. Tape Measure: Why Results Differ</h2>

      <p>
        If your smart scale reads 15% body fat while this calculator shows 22%,
        you are not alone. The two methods measure different things and have
        different error profiles.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Bioelectrical impedance (BIA) smart scales</strong> send a
        small electrical current through your body and estimate body fat from
        the resistance. BIA readings are sensitive to hydration, recent meals,
        sweat, and time of day, and consumer devices vary in how closely their
        readings align with reference methods.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>The Navy tape method</strong> uses physical circumferences
        (neck, waist, and hip for women) and a validated equation. Its
        accuracy depends on tape placement, technique, and how closely the
        person matches the population the equation was validated in.
      </p>

      <p>
        For tracking progress, pick one method and keep the conditions as
        consistent as possible — same time of day, same tape placement, same
        hydration status. Comparing absolute numbers across different methods
        is rarely useful.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Skinny Fat: When Normal BMI Hides High Body Fat</h2>

      <p>
        "Skinny fat" — clinically known as <strong>normal-weight obesity
        (NWO)</strong> — describes a body composition pattern where body weight
        and BMI fall within a normal range, but body fat percentage is high and
        skeletal muscle mass is low.
      </p>

      <p>
        Research suggests this phenotype is associated with elevated visceral
        fat and an increased risk of insulin resistance, dyslipidemia, chronic
        inflammation, and cardiovascular disease — risks that BMI alone does
        not capture. A person who is skinny fat may appear slim but carry
        metabolically harmful fat around their organs.
      </p>

      <p>
        If your calculator result shows a high body fat percentage despite a
        normal weight, resistance training and adequate protein intake are
        commonly recommended to improve body composition. Use our
        <a href="/tools/nutrition/protein-calculator/">Protein Calculator</a>
        to estimate a daily protein target.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Visceral vs. Subcutaneous Fat</h2>

      <p>
        Not all body fat behaves the same way. The two main types have
        different locations and different health implications.
      </p>

      <div class="comparison-table-wrap">
        <table>
          <caption>Differences between visceral and subcutaneous fat</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Feature</th>
              <th scope="col">Visceral Fat</th>
              <th scope="col">Subcutaneous Fat</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Location</td>
              <td>Deep in the belly, surrounding organs</td>
              <td>Just under the skin, all around the body</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Appearance</td>
              <td>Firm, "beer belly" or "apple shape"</td>
              <td>Soft and squishy, "love handles"</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Health context</td>
              <td>Frequently linked in research to high blood pressure, high cholesterol, high blood sugar, type 2 diabetes, and heart disease</td>
              <td>Less metabolically active; can still contribute to risk when excess</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        According to the Cleveland Clinic, visceral fat is associated with
        what one physician calls "the three horsemen of the apocalypse": high
        blood pressure, high cholesterol, and high blood sugar. A waist over
        <strong>40 inches (101.6 cm)</strong> for men or <strong>35 inches
        (88.9 cm)</strong> for non-pregnant women is commonly used as a signal
        of elevated risk, regardless of body fat percentage.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Example Calculation: How the Navy Formula Works</h2>

      <p>
        Consider Mike, a 30-year-old male who is 5 ft 10 in (70 in / 178 cm)
        tall, with a neck of 15 in (38.1 cm) and a waist of 34 in (86.3 cm).
      </p>

      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Step 1 — Circumference difference:</strong>
          <code>waist − neck = 34 − 15 = 19 in</code>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Step 2 — Apply the male Navy formula:</strong>
          <code>BF% = 86.010 × log₁₀(19) − 70.041 × log₁₀(70) + 36.76</code>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Step 3 — Solve:</strong>
          <code>log₁₀(19) ≈ 1.279; log₁₀(70) ≈ 1.845</code>
          <br />
          <code>BF% ≈ 86.010 × 1.279 − 70.041 × 1.845 + 36.76</code>
          <br />
          <code>BF% ≈ 110.0 − 129.2 + 36.76 ≈ 17.6%</code>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <p>
        Because the calculator works with the exact values you enter (not the
        rounded numbers shown here), your live result may differ slightly from
        the numbers above.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>How Accurate Is a Navy Body Fat Calculator?</h2>

      <p>
        The Navy circumference method is convenient because it requires only
        a tape measure, but it should not be treated as laboratory-grade
        body-composition testing.
      </p>

      <p>
        In a 2022 study of physically fit U.S. Marines, the circumference
        method underestimated body fat in men by about 2.5–2.6 percentage
        points and overestimated it in women by about 1.3–2.3 percentage
        points. Individual variability was also several percentage points.
      </p>

      <div class="callout">
        <strong>Reality check:</strong> Different body-composition methods
        have different strengths, weaknesses, and error profiles. Published
        error margins vary by study, device, population, and measurement
        conditions. No home method is exact, and DEXA itself is not a perfect
        reference standard. The trend over time is usually more meaningful
        than a single reading from any method.
      </div>

      <p>
        If you use this calculator repeatedly, keep your measurement technique,
        time of day, tape placement, and conditions as consistent as possible
        and watch the trend rather than overinterpreting a single reading.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Body Fat Measurement Methods Compared</h2>

      <p>
        Each body-composition method has different strengths and limitations.
        The table below summarizes commonly cited characteristics — actual
        error margins vary by study, device, and population.
      </p>

      <div class="comparison-table-wrap">
        <table>
          <caption>Common body fat measurement methods</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Method</th>
              <th scope="col">Typical accuracy context</th>
              <th scope="col">Best for</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
